      <div class="help-section" id="help-getting-started">
        <h3>1. Getting Started &amp; Favorites</h3>
        <p>Open OraPulse and you land on the connect screen. Fill in the DB Host/IP, Port, SID (or Instance name), an Oracle account, and its password, then click <strong>Oracle DB Connect Start</strong>. There is no separate app login -- the Oracle account you type in is the only credential involved, and it's used to connect straight to that database, on this same machine, over the network.</p>
        <p>For read access to every card, connect with a SYSTEM-level (DBA) account; a lower-privileged account still works, but any card whose underlying view it can't read shows "You do not have permission to view this." instead of blanking out the whole page.</p>
        <p>Click <strong>+ Save</strong> to store the current form's connection details (password included) as a favorite -- it's encrypted and kept only in this app's own local <code>data/</code> folder, never sent anywhere else. Next time, pick it from the dropdown at the top of the form and it connects immediately, no typing needed.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/connect-screen.png" alt="Connect screen">
          <figcaption>The connect screen: fill in connection details or pick a saved favorite.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-dashboard">
        <h3>2. DashBoard: Instance / CPU-Memory / Sessions</h3>
        <p>The DashBoard tab is what you land on right after connecting, and it's the one tab that keeps refreshing itself automatically while you're looking at it (see <a href="#help-dbload">How OraPulse Reduces DB Load</a> for the exact timing of each piece).</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Instance Information</strong> -- name, status, host, full version, startup time, and a live uptime clock.</li>
          <li><strong>CPU / Memory Usage</strong> -- Host CPU Utilization %, and SGA+PGA usage against the configured memory target.</li>
          <li><strong>Current Session List</strong> -- every real user session (background processes are excluded, and so is OraPulse's own brief monitoring connection). Use the Status/Machine/Program dropdowns to narrow it down. <strong>Right-click a row</strong> to kill that session immediately, view the SQL it's currently running, or see wait detail (including, for a file/block-based wait, which table/segment that block belongs to).</li>
          <li><strong>Blocking Session</strong> -- lists any session that's currently blocking another one.</li>
          <li><strong>Long Running Session</strong> -- progress bars for long-running operations (large table scans, backups, index builds), with elapsed time and an estimate of time remaining.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/dashboard-overview.png" alt="DashBoard tab overview">
          <figcaption>Instance Information, CPU/Memory, Current Session List, Blocking Session, and Long Running Session.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-tablestats-jobs">
        <h3>3. DashBoard: Table Statistics &amp; Scheduler/Job Failures</h3>
        <p><strong>Table Statistics Collection</strong> -- pick a schema from the dropdown to see every one of its tables' row/block counts and when each was last analyzed, oldest first, so tables overdue for a stats refresh surface at the top. Check one or more rows and click <strong>Gather Statistics for Selected</strong> to run <code>DBMS_STATS.GATHER_TABLE_STATS</code> against them right from here.</p>
        <p><strong>Scheduler/Job Failures</strong> -- two lists in one card: recent <code>DBMS_SCHEDULER</code> job runs that didn't succeed, and legacy <code>DBMS_JOB</code> jobs that are currently broken or have a nonzero failure count (a live snapshot, since that older job type keeps no per-run history). Use the schema filter to narrow either list down, and the Refresh button to check again on demand.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/dashboard-tablestats-jobs.png" alt="Table Statistics Collection and Scheduler/Job Failures cards">
          <figcaption>Table Statistics Collection (with a batch "gather stats" action) and Scheduler/Job Failures.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-security-alertlog">
        <h3>4. DashBoard: Account Security &amp; Alert Log</h3>
        <p><strong>Account Security</strong> -- currently locked accounts (locked manually, or automatically after too many failed logins), and accounts whose password has already expired or is expiring within the next few days. Both come straight from <code>DBA_USERS</code>.</p>
        <p><strong>Alert Log Analysis</strong> -- noteworthy entries (errors/incidents) from the database's own alert log over the trailing few days, most recent first.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/dashboard-security-alertlog.png" alt="Account Security and Alert Log Analysis cards">
          <figcaption>Account Security (locked accounts, expiring passwords) and Alert Log Analysis.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-ops">
        <h3>5. Ops Tab</h3>
        <p>Operational and performance detail, loosely modeled on an AWR report's sections but built entirely on license-free <code>V$</code> views -- no Diagnostics or Tuning Pack needed. Covers Key Instance Parameters, Undo Configuration &amp; Status, Instance Efficiency %, Load Profile, TEMP Tablespace Usage, TEMP Usage by Session, Datafile Autoextend Status (sorted by how close each file is to its MAXSIZE), Redo Log Status, Tablespace I/O Stats, the Top 5 queries by elapsed time / CPU time / buffer gets, and the Top 5 wait events. Click any <code>SQL_ID</code> to view that statement's full text.</p>
        <p>Every card on this tab can be dragged by its <code>::</code> handle to reorder it (even across tabs) -- your layout is remembered.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/ops-top.png" alt="Ops tab, upper section">
          <figcaption>Key Instance Parameters, Undo, Instance Efficiency, Load Profile, TEMP usage, and Datafile Autoextend.</figcaption>
        </div>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/ops-topsql-waits.png" alt="Ops tab, lower section">
          <figcaption>Redo Log Status, Tablespace I/O, Top SQL by elapsed/CPU/buffer gets, and Top Wait Events.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-recovery">
        <h3>6. Recovery Tab</h3>
        <p>Fast Recovery Area (FRA) overview and a breakdown by file type (archived logs, backup pieces, control files), plus Recent DML -- the last 50 INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/MERGE statements, most recently active first, with Type/Schema filters.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/recovery.png" alt="Recovery tab">
          <figcaption>Recent DML, Fast Recovery Area overview, and FRA usage by file type.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-tuning">
        <h3>7. Tuning Tab</h3>
        <p>A rule-based tuning advisor -- click <strong>Run Check</strong> to check buffer/library cache hit ratios, hard parse ratio, disk sorts, wait event increases, tablespace free space, invalid objects, blocking sessions, and slow SQL, all against plain <code>V$</code>/<code>DBA_</code> views. Each finding shows a severity badge (Critical/Warning/Info) and its own detail table.</p>
        <p>Rate-based checks (hit ratios, parsing, wait events) compare against the previous check's snapshot, so the very first check on a database may show "not enough history yet" for those specific items until you run it again.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/tuning.png" alt="Tuning tab showing example findings">
          <figcaption>Example findings: a Tablespace Usage warning and Invalid Objects Found.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-objview">
        <h3>8. Obj View/SQL Tab: Object Explorer</h3>
        <p>Pick a schema on the left to browse its objects, grouped by type (tables, views, packages, triggers, and more) -- objects in red are <code>INVALID</code>. Use the Type dropdown or the text filter to narrow the tree down. Click any object to fetch and display its DDL script (via <code>DBMS_METADATA.GET_DDL</code>) on the right.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/objectview-ddl.png" alt="Object View showing a table's DDL">
          <figcaption>Browsing a schema's objects and viewing a table's generated DDL.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-sqlrunner">
        <h3>9. Obj View/SQL Tab: SQL Query Runner</h3>
        <p>A read-only query runner right below the object explorer: type a single <code>SELECT</code> statement and click <strong>Run</strong>. Results are capped at 200 rows; CLOBs render as text (BLOBs are out of scope). Only <code>SELECT</code> is allowed -- there's no DML/DDL execution here, so a mistyped query can't affect the wider instance. A long-running query can be stopped with <strong>Cancel</strong> while it's still executing.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/sqlrunner.png" alt="SQL Query Runner with a result">
          <figcaption>Running a query and viewing its result.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-report">
        <h3>10. Weekly DB Health Report</h3>
        <p>Click <strong>Report</strong> (next to Refresh) to generate a self-contained HTML report -- inline SVG charts, no external dependencies -- covering the trailing days of this DB's status. A lightweight snapshot collector runs every 15 minutes in the background as soon as a connection succeeds (it keeps running even if you close the browser tab), building up the history the report's trend charts need. The finished report is written to a <code>report/&lt;YYYY-MM-DD&gt;/</code> folder next to the running app.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-lang-theme">
        <h3>11. Language &amp; Theme</h3>
        <p>The two dropdowns at the top right switch the whole UI between English and Korean, and between Dark and Light themes -- both choices are remembered for next time. Switching language re-renders every already-loaded card immediately, without re-fetching from the server.</p>
        <div class="help-shot">
          <img src="/images/help/dashboard-korean.png" alt="Dashboard in Korean">
          <figcaption>The same DashBoard tab with the language switched to Korean.</figcaption>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="help-section" id="help-dbload">
        <h3>12. How OraPulse Reduces DB Load</h3>
        <p>Instead of refreshing everything on one fixed timer, the DashBoard tab's data is split into independently-timed tiers: session list/blocking sessions every 15 seconds, CPU/memory every 30 seconds, and instance info every 5 minutes; the Ops tab refreshes every 5 minutes (only while it's the tab you're actually looking at), and Tuning every 10 minutes (same condition). All of this automatically pauses while the browser tab is hidden or minimized, and if OraPulse is open in more than one tab or window at once, only one of them actually polls -- switching back or bringing that tab into focus catches it up immediately. On top of that, the server itself briefly caches (15 seconds) an identical request against the same DB, so a manual refresh that happens to land right next to an automatic one only queries Oracle once.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

